<%# Classroom detail, rebuilt on the shared page header.

    It hand-rolled its own h1 and actions row inside two redundant `mx-auto w-full` wrappers, with
    `my-6` on the row rather than the header block's own `mb-6`. The h1 joined three facts with
    commas - name, grade and year - so the page's accessible name carried its metadata; the name is
    the title now and the rest is the description.

    The trading switch has moved twice. It started inside the h1's flex row, which put a form control
    in the heading; it then went to the header's action area, which is for the page's *actions* and is
    where it read as one. It is a setting, so it is a setting card now - see `_trading_setting`. Its
    inline `onchange="this.form.requestSubmit()"` became a Stimulus action along the way; it was the
    last inline handler in the app.

    Section order is the summary band, the setting, the roster, then the grade books - figures first
    because that is what an entity detail page opens with, and the two collections stacked full width
    rather than side by side. %>
<% content_for :title, @classroom.name %>

<div class="mx-auto w-full">
  <% meta = [
       ("Grade #{@classroom.grades_display}" if @classroom.grades_display.present?),
       @classroom.year_name
     ].compact.join(", ") %>

  <%= render layout: "components/ui/page_header",
             locals: { title: @classroom.name, description: meta } do %>
    <% if @can_manage_students %>
      <%= link_to "Add student", new_classroom_student_path(@classroom), class: "tw-btn-primary" %>
      <% if policy(@classroom).edit? %>
        <%= link_to "Edit classroom", edit_classroom_path(@classroom), class: "tw-btn-secondary" %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>

  <%# Stacked sections, each the full column width, rather than the roster and the grade book rail
      side by side. `lg:flex-row` gave one collection about 760px and the other 256px at 1366px, which
      says "the narrow one is supporting detail" - and Polaris, whose two-column layout this imitated,
      is explicit that a secondary column carries metadata rather than a second collection. Stripe's
      customer page, GitHub's repository page and Linear's project page all stack an entity's related
      collections full width and let the order say which is primary.

      space-y-6 is the section rhythm this document sets, and it is on the wrapper so each child is a
      top-level section - which is also why the grade books partial emits a single <section>.

      No `mt-6` on it. Adjacent vertical margins collapse to the larger, and the page header's own `mb-6`
      is the same 24px, so it measured nothing - and an inert margin is worse than none, because it reads
      as load-bearing and becomes load-bearing the moment anything is inserted above it. The header block
      owns this gap, everywhere. %>
  <div class="space-y-6">
    <%# No four-across stat band here, deliberately, and this is the one place the page departs from
        what the field does. Stripe leads a customer with its figures and the portfolio page in this app
        uses exactly that strip - but measured at 1366x768 it is 134px tall, and with the setting card
        above the roster it put the first student at **567px of a 625px viewport**. The roster is why a
        teacher opens this page; a summary that pushes it off screen costs more than it tells.

        ClassroomsController still computes @classroom_stats - total students, students who have traded,
        class portfolio value, orders this week - and nothing renders it, which is four queries a
        request thrown away. That is a real finding and it is recorded in design-todo rather than
        answered by putting a 134px band above the thing the page is for. A one-line meta summary beside
        the title is the shape that would fit. %>
    <%# The roster, with student trading on the line under the heading rather than in a card of its
        own. A section's control belongs on its header row - Polaris's card header action, Primer's
        Subhead, Stripe's list sections - and pairing the switch with the sentence that states the
        state is what removes any need for a status pill beside it.

        Heading, no separate description line: "Students" against "Grade books" is not the ambiguous
        case design.md's table-description rule came from, and the trading sentence is the line here. %>
    <section aria-labelledby="students-heading">
      <h2 id="students-heading" class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">Students</h2>

      <% if @can_manage_students %>
        <%= render "trading_setting", classroom: @classroom %>
      <% end %>

      <div class="mt-3">
        <%= render "classroom_students_table", students: @classroom.users %>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%= render "grade_books_list", grade_books: @classroom.grade_books %>
  </div>
</div>
